  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SUPPORTING REGIONAL HAWKSBILL RECOVERY IN THE EASTERN PACIFIC OCEAN: RE-INVIGORATING THE ICAPONETWORK AND SUSTAINING SIX TOP-TIER NESTING BEACH CONSERVATION PROJECTS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PROJECT SUMMARY HAWKSBILL TURTLES (ERETMOCHELYS IMBRICATA) WERE THOUGHT TO HAVE BEEN EXTIRPATED IN THE EASTERN PACIFIC OCEAN AS RECENTLY AS 2007, BUT THERE IS NOW RENEWED HOPE FOR RECOVERY. THE DEVELOPMENT OF THE MORE OPTIMISTIC CONSERVATION OUTLOOK FOR THE POPULATION OVER THE PAST DECADE HAS LARGELY BEEN CATALYZED BY THE EASTERN PACIFIC HAWKSBILL INITIATIVE (ICAPO), A REGIONAL NETWORK THAT HAS WORKED COLLABORATIVELY WITH NUMEROUS PARTNERS TO ESTABLISH DOZENS OF MONITORING AND CONSERVATION PROJECTS THROUGHOUT THE REGION. RECENT YEARS HAVE RESULTED IN A DRAMATIC INCREASE IN THE NUMBER OF RESEARCH AND CONSERVATION PROJECTS FOCUSED ON HAWKSBILL TURTLES IN THE EASTERN PACIFIC, WITH A CORRESPONDING INCREASE IN THE NUMBER OF INDIVIDUALS AND ORGANIZATIONS INVOLVED. THIS REALITY HAS ELEVATED THE IMPORTANCE OF ICAPOS LEADERSHIP TO PRIORITIZE RESEARCH ENDEAVORS, MAINTAIN REGIONAL PERSPECTIVES, STREAMLINE COLLABORATIONS, AND ENSURE A UNIFIED VISION THAT WILL ULTIMATELY SUPPORT HAWKSBILL RECOVERY IN THE REGION. NONETHELESS, FINANCIAL AND TEMPORAL SUPPORT DEDICATED TO MANAGING THE NETWORK ITSELF HAS BEEN LIMITED, AND THIS HAS LED TO A DECREASE IN SOME OF THE ACTIVITIES CRUCIAL TO THE ONGOING GROWTH AND EFFECTIVENESS OF ICAPO AS NEW CONSERVATION REALITIES HAVE EMERGED.</narrative>
  </description>
